{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1PospNjxtAbCbJqQHEg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. I did not write a proof, and the file is unchanged.\n\nThe statement is true: the Putnam 2021 A6 answer is \"yes, P(2) is composite.\" The difficulty is that I don't have a complete argument I could formalize. Two steps I checked are sound. Every complex root z of P satisfies |z| < 2, by a Cauchy-type bound. Since P has 0/1 coefficients, P(2) ≥ 1, so P(2) ≠ 0 and, when P is nonconstant, P(2) ≠ 1. The missing piece is the step that rules out P(2) being prime, which requires either the official argument or a substitute I haven't found. Even with that, the formalization would need Lean infrastructure for root bounds and factor analysis that is well beyond this attempt."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":449,"cache_read_input_tokens":1881,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":449,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"49ffbe69-6740-4a2a-b22e-5d7a62ddfbae","uuid":"9513af4d-5417-41eb-b45c-9b7511148c4f","timestamp":"2026-10-14T03:09:14.455Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1PosVmT5dD71dKD7Mp5"}
